<pdd-reason>The daemon that closes the loop: it drains `feature_intake` and turns surviving pitches into public `VOTING` rows. Without it a pitch reaches the queue and dies there.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"process_one","signature":"async def process_one(raw: str, supabase: AsyncClient, *, blueprint: str) -> str","returns":"outcome tag: 'inserted' | 'rejected' | 'malformed' | 'duplicate' | 'unavailable' | 'merged' | 'already_shipped' | 'postponed' | 'split'"},
  {"name":"run","signature":"async def run(stop: asyncio.Event | None = None) -> None","returns":"the BRPOP loop; returns when stop is set"},
  {"name":"main","signature":"def main() -> None","returns":"console-script entrypoint; wraps asyncio.run(run())"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/constants_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/config_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/orchestration/screener_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/orchestration/pm_agent_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/orchestration/architect_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `orchestrator/ingestion_service.py` — a long-running process, not an ASGI app.

Import the three agents by symbol — `from orchestrator.pm_agent import classify`,
`from orchestrator.screener import screen_pitch`, and
`from orchestrator.architect import decide_shape, load_blueprint` — not as modules. One import style, so a
regeneration does not silently move where the seam is.

Import the screener as `from orchestrator.screener import screen_pitch`. The Python package is
`orchestrator/`; `orchestration/` is the prompt directory and is not importable.

Build the Supabase client with `from supabase._async.client import create_client` — the installed
supabase-py exports `create_client`, not `create_async_client`, and `backend/main.py` already
binds it the same way.

<responsibility>
Consume the intake queue, screen each pitch, and persist the survivors as public feature rows.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT judge a pitch — `screener.screen_pitch` owns every verdict.
This module DOES NOT serve HTTP or import from `backend/`; Redis is the only channel between the API and this process.
This module DOES NOT write, update, or delete a `pending_pitch:*` record — the API writes it once and its TTL alone clears it.
This module DOES NOT notify an author of any outcome; the MVP reports no screening result.
This module DOES NOT judge duplication — `pm_agent.classify` owns that decision; this module only applies it.
This module DOES NOT judge buildability — `architect.decide_shape` owns that; this module applies it.
This module DOES NOT write a feature block or acceptance criteria; that is sprint-time work.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
**Intake item** — one JSON string on the `feature_intake` list, written by `POST /api/features`. Its keys are exactly `feature_id`, `author_id`, `title`, `description`, `submitted_at`.
**Survivor** — a pitch whose verdict is `passed=True`. Only survivors reach Postgres.
**Board row** — a `feature_requests` row. A pitch becomes public the moment one is inserted, so the insert is the last step of handling an item, never the first.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY `BRPOP` from `feature_intake` and insert into `feature_requests`.
- MAY read `settings` from `shared.config` and construct its own Supabase and Redis clients.
- MUST NOT write any Redis key — this process is a queue consumer, not a producer.
- MUST NOT update or delete an existing `feature_requests` row.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Consume with `BRPOP` on the key from `shared.constants.REDIS_FEATURE_INTAKE`, using a bounded timeout so a stop signal is noticed within one timeout rather than blocking forever. On timeout, loop.
R2 (MUST): Expose the expected key set as a module-level `frozenset` constant and require the parsed item's keys to equal it **exactly** — not merely contain it. The five are `feature_id`, `author_id`, `title`, `description`, `submitted_at`. A subset test passes an item carrying unknown extra fields, and neither a subset nor a superset test can be pinned from outside the module, so a producer/consumer contract test has nothing to compare against. These names are the contract between two processes that never import each other: dropping one silently strands every pitch in the queue, which has already happened once.

R3 (MUST): Treat an unparseable or non-object item as `malformed` — log at WARNING with length only, drop it, continue. One bad item MUST NOT stop the daemon, because `BRPOP` is destructive and a crash loop would drain the queue into nothing.
R4 (MUST): `await screen_pitch(...)` for every well-formed item and act only on its verdict. A rejected pitch is dropped: not inserted, not written back to Redis, not retried.
R16 (MUST): Treat `ScreeningUnavailable` as its own outcome, `unavailable` — log at ERROR with the `feature_id`, drop the item, continue. It means no verdict was obtained, so inserting would publish unscreened content and is forbidden; but it is an infrastructure failure rather than a rejected pitch, and conflating the two would hide a model outage inside a normal-looking rejection count.
R5 (MUST NOT): Persist a rejected pitch's title or description anywhere, logs included. Unscreened content lives only in Redis under the API's TTL, and that invariant is what keeps it off the public board (US-02).
R6 (MUST): Insert a survivor into `feature_requests` using the payload's own `feature_id` as the primary key, its `author_id`, status `VOTING`, and `upvotes` 1. Reusing the id is what lets the author's tray match the new row to their pending entry (US-06); a fresh uuid would orphan it.
R26 (MUST): Insert a `feature_votes` row for the pitch's author against the new feature, in the same step as the board row. The row is created with `upvotes` 1 representing the author's own vote, so without a matching vote row that vote exists only as a number — the author can then upvote their own pitch and be counted twice, and `viewer_has_voted` reads false for them. This is the same invariant R21 enforces on the merge path; both paths that add to `upvotes` must add the vote row that justifies it.
R27 (MUST): Treat a unique-violation on that vote insert as already-counted — log at INFO and continue reporting `inserted`. The board row is the durable outcome; a redelivered item must not fail because the author's vote was already recorded.
R7 (MUST): Take the status from `FeatureStatus.VOTING`, never the literal string. The enum is a physical contract with the Postgres type and the only guard against a casing drift that matches zero rows.
R8 (MUST): Treat a unique-violation on insert as `duplicate` — log at INFO and continue. A redelivered id must never raise, and the primary key is what makes the insert idempotent.
R9 (MUST NOT): Set `merge_count`, `extends_id`, `parent_id`, `split_depth`, or `unlock_threshold`. Each belongs to a later phase, and a value invented here would be indistinguishable from a real dedup or split result.
R10 (MUST): Log one line per item at INFO carrying `feature_id` and the outcome tag, never the pitch text. This log is the only visibility into the daemon and is what shows a queue draining during a demo.
R11 (MUST): Build the Supabase and Redis clients once at startup — a client per item would open a connection per pitch. On shutdown close Redis with `await aclose()` and leave the Supabase client alone: it has no public close, and `auth.sign_out()` is not one — that is a network call ending a user session, which a service-role client does not have.
R12 (MUST): Handle `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` by setting the stop event and letting the in-flight item finish, so shutdown never abandons a pitch between its `BRPOP` and its insert.
R13 (MUST): Keep an unexpected exception on one item from killing the loop — log it with the `feature_id`, drop the item, continue. The daemon's uptime is what the whole pipeline depends on.
R14 (MUST): Expose `main()` as a sync wrapper over `asyncio.run(run())` with an `if __name__ == "__main__"` guard, so it runs as `python -m orchestrator.ingestion_service`. It is a daemon and MUST NOT be started under uvicorn.
R15 (MUST): Take the Redis URL and Supabase credentials from `settings`; `shared/config.py` is the only module that reads the environment.
R17 (MUST): After a passing verdict, `await pm_agent.classify(...)` with the current backlog and shipped sets, and route its four outcomes. This is the seam step 1 reserved: screening decides whether a pitch may be published, dedup decides what publishing it means.
R18 (MUST): Read the candidate sets from Postgres before classifying — `VOTING` rows as `backlog`, `COMPILED` rows as `shipped` — selecting only `id`, `title`, and `description`. Sending vote counts or status invites the model to reason about popularity, which is not what it is asked (pm_agent R11).
R19 (MUST): On `new_unique`, insert exactly as before. On `extends_shipped`, insert the same row plus `extends_id` = the target and `extends_title` = the target's title. The denormalised title is what lets a board card render the 'builds on' chip without a second query, and it is the only reason that column exists.
R20 (MUST): On `duplicate`, insert **no** new row. Instead increment the canonical row's `upvotes` by calling `increment_upvotes` with exactly one argument, `{"row_id": <canonical id>}` — it adds one and takes no `inc`/`amount` parameter, and PostgREST rejects an invented one with `PGRST202` at runtime, increment its `merge_count`, and insert a `feature_votes` row for the pitch's author against the canonical id.
R21 (MUST): Write that `feature_votes` row on every merge. Without it the merging author is counted in `upvotes` but has no vote row, so `viewer_has_voted` reads false and they can upvote the canonical feature again — one person, two votes. This is the invariant that makes a merged vote indistinguishable from a direct one.
R22 (MUST): Treat a unique-violation on that vote insert as already-counted — log at INFO and continue, still reporting `merged`. The author had already voted for the canonical row before pitching a duplicate of it, so their demand is recorded exactly once, which is the correct end state.
R23 (MUST): On `already_shipped`, insert nothing and report `already_shipped`. The feature exists; adding a row would invite the community to vote for work already done.
R24 (MUST NOT): Let a dedup failure lose a pitch. `classify` falls back to `new_unique` by contract, so an outage produces a possibly-duplicate row rather than silence — and if it ever raises, treat it as `new_unique` here rather than dropping the item.
R25 (MUST): Report each outcome under its own tag — `inserted`, `merged`, `already_shipped` — never collapsing them into one. A merge and an insert are different things happening to the community's demand signal, and a single counter would hide which the pipeline is actually doing.
R28 (MUST): Call `decide_shape` only when a row is actually going to be created — after `new_unique` or `extends_shipped`, never for `duplicate` or `already_shipped`. Those two write no feature row, so shaping them would spend a reasoning-model call to decide the status of something that does not exist.
R29 (MUST): Load the blueprint once at startup via `load_blueprint()` and pass it to every `decide_shape` call. Reading the target prompt file per item adds a filesystem round-trip to every pitch, and the file changes only when a sprint compiles.
R30 (MUST): Insert with the status the shape returns — `VOTING`, `SPLIT`, or `POSTPONED_CONFLICT` — never a hardcoded `VOTING`. This is the whole point of the stage: the community must not be asked to vote on something that cannot be built.
R31 (MUST): Write the shape's `explanation` to `ai_explanation` on the row whenever the status is not `VOTING`. It is the only thing an author is ever told about why their feature was postponed or split, and a `POSTPONED_CONFLICT` row with a null explanation is indistinguishable from a bug.
R32 (MUST): On `SPLIT`, insert the parent with status `SPLIT` and then one `VOTING` child row per returned child, each carrying `parent_id` = the parent's id and inheriting the parent's `author_id`. Inheriting the author is what keeps the children in their originator's tray (US-06); an orphaned child is a feature nobody can trace back to the idea it came from.
R33 (MUST): Give split children `upvotes` 0 and no vote row, and keep the author's single vote on the parent. The author pitched one idea, so they get one vote — seeding each of three children with a vote would turn one person into three votes and break the invariant that every upvote is backed by a vote row.
R34 (MUST): Treat a `decide_shape` failure as `VOTING` and continue. The contract already falls back that way; if it raises anyway, a screened and deduped pitch must still reach the board rather than be lost to an architect outage.
R35 (MUST): Report `postponed` and `split` as their own outcome tags. A postponed feature and a votable one are different results for the author, and collapsing them into `inserted` hides how much of the board the gate is holding back.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
**The dedup seam (US-03).** `pm_agent` will eventually classify a survivor as new-unique,
duplicate, extension, or already-shipped, and only the first two become rows. Step 1 inserts
directly instead. The seam is R4→R6: today the verdict leads straight to an insert; when US-03
lands, a classify call sits between them and R6 becomes the new-unique branch. Nothing else here
changes, which is why the insert is deliberately the last statement of an item's handling rather
than interleaved with screening.

**No outcome is reported to the author.** A rejected pitch leaves its `pending_pitch:*` record
untouched, so the tray keeps reading "screening" until the 900s TTL expires and the entry
disappears. That is the scope decision taken in US-06, and it is why R4 has no notification branch
and this module writes no Redis key at all. Reversing it later means publishing to
`REDIS_SCREENING_RESULTS` here and having the backend relay it.

**At-least-once, not exactly-once.** `BRPOP` removes an item before it is processed, so a crash
between pop and insert loses that pitch permanently; the author sees it vanish when the TTL
lapses rather than hang forever. A reliable queue (`BLMOVE` onto a processing list, or a stream
with acknowledgements) is the upgrade path — not worth the complexity for the demo, and R8 keeps
the cheap half of the guarantee.
</pdd>

